    "collapsible-content": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "faq-0": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why does the texture change between bottles — or even within the same bottle?",
            "row_content": "<p>Cold-pressed virgin coconut oil firms below 76°F. In a cool bathroom the lotion thickens; in a warm one it loosens. Give the bottle a shake before the first pump if it's been stored cool. Refined oils are uniform precisely because the lab strips out the variation — along with most of what makes the oil work. We accept the inconsistency because the absorption is the point.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        },
        "faq-1": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is the Pure Unscented variation actually unscented?",
            "row_content": "<p>There's a faint coconut and jojoba scent — that's the actual ingredients, not added fragrance, and it fades within minutes of application. We don't add masking agents to neutralize it, because masking agents are themselves fragrance compounds. If you're sensitive to synthetic fragrance, this is the variation to start with.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        },
        "faq-2": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why no synthetic fragrance?",
            "row_content": "<p>Synthetic fragrance is the leading cause of contact dermatitis in personal-care products. \"Fragrance\" or \"parfum\" is a regulatory umbrella that can hide hundreds of undisclosed compounds — the customer reading the label has no way to know what's in there. Our scented variations use named organic essential oils only, listed individually on the back panel.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        },
        "faq-3": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Will this clog my pores?",
            "row_content": "<p>Jojoba is structurally close to skin's own sebum and absorbs without forming an occlusive film, which is why we built the formula around it. Mineral oil and petrolatum — the cheap moisturizing bases in most commercial lotion — sit on top of skin and can trap debris underneath. We don't use them. If you're acne-prone, the unscented variation is the most conservative choice.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        },
        "faq-4": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How is this preserved without parabens?",
            "row_content": "<p>Organic grapefruit seed extract acts as a broad-spectrum natural preservative — the active polyphenol fraction inhibits bacterial and fungal growth in water-containing formulas. Parabens and phenoxyethanol are cheaper and more shelf-stable, which is why nearly every commercial lotion uses them. Both are also the most-asked-about ingredients by label-reading customers, which tells you something.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        },
        "faq-5": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why is the lotion slightly tinted?",
            "row_content": "<p>That's the unrefined red palm oil. It retains its naturally occurring beta-carotene, which gives the oil its red-orange color and contributes antioxidant activity to the formula. Refined palm oil is bleached white and stripped of the carotenes — cheaper, color-neutral, and missing the compounds we're using it for. The tint doesn't transfer to skin or clothing.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        },
        "faq-6": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Can I use this on my face?",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes. The oils are non-occlusive and the ingredient list is short enough that there's nothing in here we'd hesitate to put on facial skin. That said, this is formulated as a body lotion — if you have a dedicated facial routine, it'll integrate fine, but we don't make claims about it replacing a targeted face product.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        },
        "faq-palm-oil": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is the red palm oil sustainably sourced?",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes — we use RSPO-certified red palm oil. The Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil certifies the palm comes from operations meeting strict standards: no deforestation, no peatland conversion, fair labor. The \"red\" matters too — refined palm oil is cheaper but the refining strips out the carotenoids and tocotrienols we use it for. Unrefined keeps the antioxidants intact.<\/p>",
            "page": "",
            "enable_highlight": false,
            "highlight_style": "marker"
          }
        }
      },
